AB  - One among the most crucial concerns in the domain of Natural Language Processing (NLP) is the Multi-Document Summarization (MDS) and in recent decades, the focus on this issue has risen massively. Hence, it is vital for the NLP community to provide effective and reliable MDS methods. Current deep learning-dependent MDS techniques rely on the extraordinary capacity of neural networks, in order to extract distinctive features. Motivated by this fact, we introduce a novel MDS technique, named as Customized Long Short-Term Memory-based Multi-Document Summarization using IBi-GRU (CLSTM-MDS+IBi-GRU), which includes the following working processes. Firstly, the input data gets converted into tokens by the Bi-directional Transformer (BERT) tokenizer. The features, such as Term Frequency- Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF), Bag of Words (BoW), thematic features and an improved aspect term-based feature are then extracted afterwards. Finally, the summarization process takes place by utilizing the concatenation of Customized Long Short-Term Memory (CLSTM) with a pre-eminent layer. Accurate and high-quality summary is provided via introducing this layer in the LSTM module and the Bi-GRU-based Inception module (IBi-GRU), which can capture long range dependences through parallel convolution. The outcomes of this work prove the superiority of our CLSTM-MDS in the Multi-Document Summarization task.
