AB  - This paper investigates the recursive quadratic filtering problem for a class of linear discrete-time systems with token bucket protocols subject to random parameter matrices and non-Gaussian noises. In the considered communication scenario, the token bucket protocol is explicitly incorporated by modeling token consumption as a stochastic process that captures random variations in the size of transmitted measurements. Measurement signals are transmitted to the filter only when the accumulated tokens are sufficient to meet the required consumption. The original state and measurement vectors are augmented by incorporating their second-order Kronecker products, resulting in an augmented system. The joint statistical properties of the resulting noise processes and the higher-order statistics of the associated random parameters are comprehensively examined. An upper bound of the filtering error covariance is derived by solving a set of matrix difference equations, and the filter gain is subsequently optimized to minimize this bound. In addition, sufficient conditions are established to ensure the boundedness of the filtering error covariance within both lower and upper bounds. Finally, simulation experiments are conducted to validate the effectiveness of the designed quadratic filtering algorithm.
